WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.684
Three simple code lookups.

2
00:00:02.884 --> 00:00:03.866
Four turns.

3
00:00:04.066 --> 00:00:06.425
294k tokens.

4
00:00:06.625 --> 00:00:11.049
Every turn, an AI coding agent reads its
whole conversation again.

5
00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:14.936
Now the same lookups, with PeterGanuRusty.

6
00:00:15.156 --> 00:00:16.195
One question.

7
00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:17.356
One answer.

8
00:00:17.576 --> 00:00:24.257
The stream shortens to 134k tokens. 54%
fewer, in 2 turns.

9
00:00:25.384 --> 00:00:27.103
It gives an agent four things.

10
00:00:27.403 --> 00:00:31.065
Where a function is defined, and what
calls it, in one lookup.

11
00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:34.676
Search by meaning: describe a job, get the
function.

12
00:00:34.976 --> 00:00:37.217
What a change reaches, before the edit.

13
00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:40.080
And findings, remembered between sessions.

14
00:00:41.415 --> 00:00:48.178
To measure it, we ran 884 agents on one
Python repository of 86 files.

15
00:00:48.478 --> 00:00:53.978
Three questions, graded against the syntax
tree, on three model classes: light, mid

16
00:00:53.978 --> 00:00:54.567
and top.

17
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:58.625
14 ways of finding code, ranked. The best
one is marked.

18
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:02.823
Runs fully correct: 3 of 3. With no index,
2 of 3.

19
00:01:04.271 --> 00:01:08.693
The limits are real: one repository, a few
runs for each setup.

20
00:01:09.143 --> 00:01:11.596
The study measures the first two things
only.

21
00:01:12.046 --> 00:01:14.532
PeterGanuRusty. Private preview.
